#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060910 :tutorial/content "Consider the vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\langle -2,4 \\rangle\n$$\n\nshown below.\n\n![](images/cd/cd458eeb41ee09d6a4f58bc343575d46e3d362dc3edca40a47e1a7381fcc9bd2.png)\n\nExpressed in component form, our vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=-2\\mathbf{i} + 4 \\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nTo find the magnitude (length) of $\\mathbf{a}$, we can use the Pythagorean theorem on $\\triangle OXA$.\n\nThe legs of the triangle have lengths\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}OX^(→) & &= \\end{vmatrix}- 2|= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}XA^(→) & &= \\end{vmatrix}4|= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the Pythagorean theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid a \\mid &= \\sqrt{\\begin{vmatrix}OX^(→) & ^{2} +\\left.\\end{vmatrix}XA^(→)\\right|^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2^{2} + 4^{2}} \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2c0b8c99-c0fd-5bb5-b00d-033a3d589c6d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2918 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060911 :tutorial/content "We just saw that the magnitude of the vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\langle -2,4 \\rangle\n$$\n\nwas given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid a \\mid &= \\sqrt{(-2)^{2} + 4^{2}} \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, given a vector\n$\\begin{bmatrix}a_{x} \\\\ a_{y}\\end{bmatrix}$\nthe magnitude of the vector can always be computed as\n\n$$\n\\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, \\mid = \\sqrt{a_x^2+a_y^2}\n$$\n\n**Note**\n\nWe sometimes use the notation $\\mid \\mid \\mathbf a \\mid \\mid$ to denote the magnitude of a vector." :tutorial/id #uuid "60a2e291-183e-53fd-ae24-4840833f4b38" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2919 :tutorial/title "General Formula"}]}
